Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 09:20:25 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11040/11362] Retract the controlled pair: I compared two
 versions of the linter

Chasing my own open question produced the answer and retracts more than it
confirms. A fresh linter run does not flag exp6967 at all -- check_duration_vs_claim
returns no flags and summarize_artifact reads clean, exit 0 -- yet at 07:35Z I
pasted its output reading CRITICAL. Both readings were accurate when taken.

The artifact has not changed since 07:32Z. The linter has: commit 85ff82bc29,
"Claim-provenance-aware duration verification", landed 10:54:58Z, three hours
after my observation. A conductor task fixed the false positive on its own. The
defect was real when documented and is now gone.

Which means the 13:00Z controlled pair was not one. I compared exp6967's stored
stamp, written under the old rule, against exp6976's live re-check under the new
rule, and read the difference as evidence about marker counts when it was
evidence about which rule ran. The 1,628-versus-1,477 comparison is meaningless
and so is the refutation I built on it.

The transferable part: a stored flagged_adversarial stamp and a live re-check
come from different linter versions whenever the linter changed in between.
summarize_artifact prints them side by side so a reader notices the
disagreement, and I read a property of time as a property of the artifacts.
Check git log on adversarial_verify.py between the stamp and the read before
treating that disagreement as a finding.

What survives is worth acting on. exp6967 still carries flagged_adversarial True
from a rule that no longer flags it. Stamps are durable, the rules that produce
them are not, and nothing re-validates stamps when the linter changes -- so a
corrected check leaves a real result permanently excluded from every capstone and
evidence table. Clearing it needs an operator, because unstamping is precisely
what the determination-preservation lint refuses.

